  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nToday started what is expected to be a prolonged stretch of quiet \nand dry weather. The synoptic high pressure system over the south \ncentral CONUS finally has some company with a small trough situated \nover southern Minnesota. The trough however is not forecast to \ntangibly affect the weather over the forecast area. Unlike the last \nfew days, the high pressure extends to the surface, all but ending \nany precipitation chances for the next few days. Thanks to the cold \nfront and lingering CAA, highs this weekend will be a cooler break \nfrom the triple digits residing in the 90s. Still hot but within \nwhat is expected for SW Kansas in early August. \n\nBy Monday, the heat returns with highs in the triple digits and as \nhigh as 107 in the western counties. At this moment, it looks like \nthe hottest day of the stretch. The aspect that warrants monitoring \nwhether the apparent temperatures reach the 105 mark for Heat \nAdvisory criteria. For the Monday temperatures, the hottest areas \nflirt with the line. After Monday, only Barber county currently \nreaches the 105 line. This will be monitored for chances, especially \nif the ensembles continue to be a couple degrees too cool in this \npattern. By Tuesday morning, ensembles have precipitation chances at \naround 10-20% in the western counties. With the low chances and \nuncertainty, not much more information can be gleaned from the \nsignal.",
